※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4531

※ What a reference names has to outlive the reference, and nothing in the
※ language yet says how long anything lives.  Handing one back to the caller is
※ exactly the case where the compiler cannot tell: the place may be this call's
※ own storage, which is gone the moment it answers.  So a reference may be a
※ parameter, a local and a field, and may not leave the call.
※
※ Rust answers the same question with lifetimes, which say that what comes back
※ lives as long as what went in; C and Odin do not ask it at all, and a
※ reference to a local that has gone is a program nobody notices is wrong.
※ Lifetime annotations are what will lift this.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn first(a: &mut u8) → &mut u8:
    a
